  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1137 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAn upper disturbance will lift out of the Southwest Friday and\ninto the Plains this weekend, topping/suppressing the ridge aloft.\nAs it does so, chances for showers and some storms will spread\nacross the forecast area. Rains are expected to be more of the off\nand on character through the weekend and not particularly heavy.\nThe latest guidance suggests that Saturday night into Sunday will\nhave the best chances.\n\nThe focus then turns to next week. Confidence is fairly high that\na widespread rainfall event will occur Tuesday into Wednesday. An\nupper trough and associated surface low pressure and cold front\nare expected to shift east toward the Plains next week. There are\ndetails to be ironed out in the forecast, however. While the\nmajority of the multi-model ensemble solutions show a more\nprogressive wave sweeping across the country next week, a\nsignificant portion of the data (around 33%) is in one cluster\nthat is quite a bit slower with the eastward progression. Slower\nsolutions would prolong rain chances at least into Thursday and \npotentially increase rainfall amounts. A slower solution could\nalso ingest the remnant moisture from Polo in the Pacific, and\nincrease the potential for heavy rainfall.\n\nIn summary, confidence is high that it will rain and get cooler\nnext week. Confidence is low on how long it will rain and how\nmuch.\n\nLacy",
